    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48197.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it was a dumb argument. And, you know, the professor is right. If people should go back and read the 1982 Nixon decision because the Supreme Court makes a compelling argument. This was the Burger Court at the time. And they pointed out the severe chilling effect on presidential decision making that might trigger paralysis if a president has to look over his shoulder at every critical decision he's making and worry about future prosecution and then have to vet everything through a committee of lawyers instead of acting precipitously as commander-in-chief which is his duty. So that's why I think the reasoning of that precedent applies to criminal prosecutions as well.",
